# Schema: compose-socket-proxy.yml
#
# Documents the compose wiring for the socket proxy so an agent with no
# prior restrict-docker-api-access knowledge can work with the file.

## Structure

YAML with `services:` and `networks:` top-level keys (Docker Compose v2).

## services.socket-proxy

| Field | Meaning | Rule |
|-------|---------|------|
| image | tecnativa/restrict-docker-api-access, pinned `@sha256:` | Digest REQUIRED (R-4) |
| environment | One var per enabled endpoint group, value `1` | Deny-by-default (R-2); each entry needs a comment justification (R-3) |
| volumes | Exactly `/var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro` | Read-only REQUIRED (R-6) |
| networks | The internal network only | No `ports:` key may exist (R-5) |
| networks (P-7 variant) | Mapping form with `ipv4_address:` instead of a plain list entry | Only when a `network_mode: host` consumer needs P-7 (see host-network-consumers module); commented out by default |
| restart | `unless-stopped` | Proxy is infrastructure; restart is always safe |

## networks.<name>

- `driver: bridge`
- `internal: true` preferred: blocks outbound masquerading. Drop only if
  the same network must carry routed egress for another service.
- `ipam.config[].subnet` — commented out by default; required only to
  support P-7's static `ipv4_address` (a fixed subnet is a prerequisite
  for a static address — Docker rejects one on an auto-assigned subnet).
  Pick a subnet that doesn't collide with any existing network on the
  host before uncommenting.

## Validation checks

1. `grep -c "ports:" compose-socket-proxy.yml` is 0.
2. Every `environment:` entry has a trailing comment with a justification.
3. The image reference contains `@sha256:`.
4. If any consumer needs P-7: `ipam.config` and the proxy's
   `ipv4_address` are both present, uncommented, and the address falls
   inside the declared subnet.
